---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import MasonXMonogram from '../../components/MasonXMonogram.astro';
import TitleBlock from '../../components/TitleBlock.astro';

const title = "We All Label Shop Drawings in Bluebeam by Hand. Why? | TMM Blog";
const description = "The masonry shop drawing workflow everyone uses: export the rendering, drop it in Bluebeam, label every opening and course by hand. The drawing should label itself.";
const canonical = "https://themasonrymodeler.com/blog/bluebeam-shop-drawings/";
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical}>
  <MasonXMonogram />
  <TitleBlock sheet="B1.5 — Bluebeam Shop Drawings" ctaLabel="Join Waitlist →" ctaHref="/#waitlist" />

  <div class="section-divider">
    <span>Field Notes · B1.5</span>
    <span class="scale-marks"></span>
    <span>Shop Drawings</span>
  </div>

  <section class="blog-post-hero">
    <div class="blog-post-hero-inner">
      <div class="post-kicker">Field Notes</div>
      <h1>
        We All Label Shop Drawings in Bluebeam by Hand.
        <span class="accent">Why?</span>
      </h1>
      <div class="post-meta">
        <span><strong>Ryan Shanley</strong></span>
        <span>June 2026</span>
        <span>4 min read</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="blog-post-body">

    <p>
      Here's a workflow every masonry estimator knows by heart. You take the rendering of your
      wall. You drop it into Bluebeam. And you start labeling — every opening, every course,
      every joint, by hand.
    </p>

    <p>
      It's the most normal thing in the world. It's also, when you stop and look at it,
      completely backwards.
    </p>

    <h2>The markup nobody questions</h2>

    <p>
      The rendering already knows where the openings are. It drew them. The coursing is right
      there on the screen — you can count it. The bond beam courses, the lifts, the control
      joints, they're all in the picture.
    </p>

    <p>
      But the labels don't come with it. So you export a picture, open Bluebeam, and spend an
      afternoon typing back the information the model already had. Door 6 by 6-8. Course 13. CJ
      here. You're not adding anything the wall didn't already know. You're transcribing.
    </p>

    <p>
      Multiply that across every elevation on a real job. That's a day. Sometimes two. On
      something the software could have handed you finished.
    </p>

    <h2>Why it's like this</h2>

    <p>
      The reason is simple once you see it: the thing that made the rendering was never built to
      make a shop drawing. It made a picture of a wall. A nice one. But a picture isn't a
      submittal.
    </p>

    <p>
      So the work falls to you. You export the picture and annotate it by hand until it's a
      document the architect will stamp. The markup is you finishing the tool's job — doing by
      hand the part it didn't do.
    </p>

    <h2>Bluebeam isn't the problem</h2>

    <p>
      None of this is a knock on Bluebeam. Bluebeam's a great tool. For markup, RFIs,
      coordinating across trades, redlining a set — it earns its place on every estimator's
      screen.
    </p>

    <p>
      The problem isn't the tool you're marking up in. It's that you're re-typing labels the
      model already knew. Bluebeam should be where you coordinate the drawing — not where you
      build it from scratch.
    </p>

    <h2>The drawing should label itself</h2>

    <p>
      Here's what I wanted instead. Tag every block as you build the wall — its finish, its type,
      the opening it frames, the lift it sits in. Then the elevation comes out labeled. Courses
      numbered. Openings called out. Bond beams and control joints in position. Finishes banded.
      Submittal-ready, the moment the wall exists.
    </p>

    <p>
      No export. No round trip. No afternoon transcribing. The drawing knows what it is because
      every block told it.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        I shouldn't be marking up a picture of my own wall. The wall already knows every block.
        The drawing should too.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      That's why <a href="/masonry-shop-drawings">masonry shop drawings</a> aren't a separate step
      in The Masonry Modeler — they're a byproduct of building the wall. Tag it once, and the shop
      drawing, the count, and the order are the same wall.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you've got a Bluebeam session open right now labeling courses by hand, that's the
      afternoon I'm trying to give you back.
    </p>

    <a href="/#waitlist" class="post-cta-inline">Join the waitlist →</a>

  </article>

  <div class="blog-post-footer">
    <a href="/blog/" class="blog-back-link">← All posts</a>
  </div>

</Layout>
